/src/ops/task.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/task/directory.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/task/file_save.rs","rust/loopflow/src/session.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/children.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/sqlite.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/sqlite/children.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/sqlite/sessions.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/sqlite/task_work.rs","rust/loopflow/tests/dto_fixtures.rs","scripts/benchmarks/desktop-performance/README.md","scripts/loopflow-dev.py","swift/Loopflow/Models/MultiplexerLayout.swift","swift/Loopflow/Models/MultiplexerStore.swift","swift/Loopflow/Models/SessionRecord.swift","swift/Loopflow/Models/TaskFlow.swift","swift/Loopflow/Models/TaskWorkspace.swift","swift/Loopflow/Models/Wave.swift","swift/Loopflow/Models/WaveViewModel.swift","swift/Loopflow/Models/WaveWorkMap.swift","swift/Loopflow/Models/WorktreeLayout.swift","swift/Loopflow/Services/RegistryQuery.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Loopflow.sdef","swift/LoopflowMac/MacLocalWaveAgentLauncher.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/PodiumModel.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Services/Ghostty/GhosttyManager.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Services/Perf.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/TaskFileDocument.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/RoadmapView.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/SessionsView.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/TaskFilesView.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/TaskFlowView.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/TaskWorkspaceView.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/WaveDetailPane.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/WavesView.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/WorkSurfaceView.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/WorkspaceBreadcrumbBar.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/WorkspaceNavigator.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/WorkspaceProjection.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/DesktopHeadlessTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/DesktopPerformanceTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/GhosttyTerminalInputTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/LocalWaveAgentLauncherTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/MultiplexerStoreTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/PodiumModelTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/SessionChromeProofTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/SessionsPolishTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/SessionsStoreTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/TaskCommentsProofTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/TaskFilesTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/TaskFlowProofTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/TaskMonitorProofTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/TaskMonitorTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/TaskRunsProofTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/TaskSessionLaunchProofTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/WaveRowTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/WaveTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/WorkspaceDestinationTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/WorkspaceNavigationProofTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/WorkspaceNavigationTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/WorktreeWorkspaceTests.swift","swift/README.md","tests/fixtures/dto/README.md","tests/fixtures/dto/flow_catalog.json","tests/fixtures/dto/flow_numeric_nested.json","tests/fixtures/dto/flow_template.json","tests/fixtures/dto/roadmap_snapshot.json","tests/fixtures/dto/session.json","tests/fixtures/dto/session_membership_graphs.json","tests/fixtures/dto/session_memberships.json","tests/fixtures/dto/session_metadata.json","tests/fixtures/dto/session_page.json","tests/fixtures/dto/sessions.json","tests/fixtures/dto/task_checkout.json","tests/fixtures/dto/task_condition_states.json","tests/fixtures/dto/task_files.json","tests/fixtures/dto/task_flow.json","tests/fixtures/dto/task_flow_stalled.json","tests/fixtures/dto/wave_detail.json","wave/growth/GOAL.md","wave/infrastructure/GOAL.md","wave/product/GOAL.md","wave/product/MEMORY.md"]}],"merged_prs":[{"number":1300,"title":"Navigate work with ⌘K and Task links, and inspect folded Flow templates","body":"Finding work in Desktop required navigating the sidebar, and Flow previews exposed every composed step at once. This PR adds searchable workspace destinations, direct Task links, and expandable Flow templates so you can reach work and inspect its plan more easily.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Workspace UX after the data model: orphan control room, bind, Flow views, ⌘K, dark mode · LOO-303](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-303/workspace-ux-after-the-data-model-orphan-control-room-bind-flow-views)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Add a repository-scoped ⌘K palette for Waves, Tasks, Sessions, Flows, and contextual actions, with recent destinations first. **Bind to Task…** reuses the existing permanent-assignment picker.\n- Open `loopflow://task/ISSUE` links through exact `lf roadmap --task` lookup, including retained and completed Tasks. Opening details starts no work; ambiguous links offer repository choices, and failed reads preserve the workspace.\n- Fold composed Flows in Wave details, Task previews, and the Flow catalog. Keyboard disclosure preserves nested alternatives, repeated and empty groups, and named return targets. Template structure and execution graphs share Rust resolution; captured FlowSessions retain their expanded graphs.\n- Keep palette input out of retained terminals, restore focus on Escape, and prevent hidden terminals from taking keyboard focus.\n\nPalette search includes current Tasks and visited historical Tasks; it does not search all Task history.\n\n## Checks\n\nRecorded branch verification passed formatting, all-target Clippy, 2,023 Rust tests, 78 website tests, and the Desktop build. A chapter-transfer regression found by the Desktop suite was repaired; all 35 focused Desktop tests then passed. The full Desktop suite was not repeated after that repair. Native Window/Metal/PTY diagnostics and configured workspace acceptance remain unverified on the final branch.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough, not performed on the final branch:\n\n1. Press ⌘K, search for a Task identifier, and press Return. Its details should open without starting work.\n2. Open `loopflow://task/ISSUE` using an existing identifier. A retained Task should remain inspectable outside the current chapter.\n3. Select a Flow in ⌘K and expand a composed group with the keyboard. Inspect a return step to see its target even when the target’s group is folded.","files":[],"additions":2441,"deletions":83,"changed_files":26,"merge_commit":"de074a2ebd2cb54e6f7dde799400dae36c87bbbe"},{"number":1354,"title":"Inspect Linear Tasks before starting and adopt existing branches","body":"Task status previously required a local execution record, and checkout rejected branches that already existed. This PR makes Linear Tasks inspectable before starting work and lets checkout reuse existing branches, dirty worktrees, and open PRs while preserving saved Flow progress.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Resolve Tasks from Linear and Git across Homes, and run workers on the official lf · LOO-334](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-334/resolve-tasks-from-linear-and-git-across-homes-and-run-workers-on-the)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Share repository-scoped issue and Project records between exact Task lookup and Wave views. Provider revisions prevent older observations from overwriting newer facts; failed refreshes retain the last successful observation and its timestamp.\n- Return planning state, freshness, errors, and optional execution from `lf task status`. Project-less issues remain inspectable, inspection allocates no Task execution or worktree, and observing a merged PR no longer completes the Task.\n- Let checkout and run adopt Linear's branch, reuse an existing worktree, or fetch an open PR's remote branch. Adoption retains the PR without editing it; repeated operations preserve the captured Flow and cursor.\n- Recheck planning before managed continuation and at driver boundaries. Removed, terminal, unavailable, or mismatched planning stops advancement while preserving progress. Independent worktree Flows remain available.\n- Select and verify the installed runtime for Flow children and review launchers, with source execution fallbacks on uninstalled machines.\n\n## Compatibility and limits\n\n`lf task status --json` now returns a planning/execution envelope; Rust and Swift consumers and fixtures are updated together. Planning migrations cover the released schema, not intermediate branch schemas.\n\nConflicting Project Team/Initiative relationships remain unresolved and block managed execution. Stored change receipts do not establish live webhook delivery.\n\n## Checks\n\nAdded coverage for planning-only CLI inspection, provider failures and revisions, removal races, released-schema migration, and Task adoption with saved progress. Adoption coverage uses real Git and CLI operations with simulated Linear/GitHub responses. Tests were reviewed but not rerun for this description; this is not live-provider validation.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough: in a connected repository, run `lf task status <issue> --json` for an issue with no local execution. It should show planning with `execution: null` and leave worktrees unchanged.\n\nFor an issue whose Linear branch already has a dirty worktree and open PR, run `lf task checkout <issue>`. It should reuse that worktree, preserve the edits, and retain the PR. Repeating checkout should retain the same placement and any saved Flow progress. This walkthrough has not been performed against live providers.","files":[],"additions":4516,"deletions":1044,"changed_files":60,"merge_commit":"88bd7919b22078dec980739a068493ab1b478336"},{"number":1356,"title":"Organize CLI commands by owner and add work and account overviews","body":"Organize PR commands under Task, inspection under Monitor, accounts under Account, and release operations under Repo. Unique shorthand keeps commands such as `lf land`, `lf ps`, and `lf top` available without predecessor aliases. Add work and account overviews with reasons, next actions, and explicit missing evidence; carry access selection through child launches and document first local setup.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Implement the CLI owner tree and the monitor and identity overviews · LOO-338](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-338/implement-the-cli-owner-tree-and-the-monitor-and-identity-overviews)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\nIntegrate the shared Task-work reader and one-Home store APIs. Monitor observation and Session history use shared Home resolution. Update CLI fixtures and regenerate the command reference to remove retired installation options.\n\nRepair CI failures from bff65e3fee5e3eb48a8864570b4b47df3e7b48a6: graph assertions now expect the canonical `task sync` label, and the cross-checkout managed-review fixture binds its Team and Initiative before Task creation. TESTING.md requires these checks for CLI owner-tree changes.\n\nRepair validation: reproduced all three graph failures and the installation failure before fixing them. `cargo test -p loopflow --lib engine::flow_graph::tests --jobs 4` passed all 7 tests; `uv run python scripts/test_task_installation.py` passed all 6 disposable Linux installation proofs plus its planning migration check; `cargo clippy --all-targets --jobs 4 -- -D warnings`, `cargo fmt --all -- --check`, and `git diff --check` passed. Full hosted CI remains pending on the repaired head. Installation proofs use simulated providers; they do not establish live-provider lifecycle behavior.\n\nReview: preserve the graph topology and saved-review assertions, reuse the existing planning fixture helper, and change no production behavior for these CI repairs.","files":[],"additions":5852,"deletions":4678,"changed_files":201,"merge_commit":"a55818079098db6d2b09c4652cbdb3db2d8b6eec"},{"number":1369,"title":"Keep Task Sessions, terminals and file drafts together across visits","body":"Returning to a Task previously meant navigating separate conversation, terminal and file views. Tasks now open retained workspaces that bring their Sessions together and preserve shells, file drafts and split layouts while switching work.\n\n## What changes\n\n- Group Sessions by resolved checkout and owning Home, including subdirectories and symlink aliases. Preserve explicit Task bindings and keep repo/Wave Sessions separate. Task navigation shows Sessions regardless of attention metadata or provider mode; orphan inventory remains available through Debug and `lf session list --orphan`.\n- Let Session selection focus or replace a pane without rearranging other splits. Command-click toggles visibility; Option-click opens alongside. New arrivals preserve focus, and hiding panes keeps processes alive.\n- Browse checkout files and save revision-checked edits without a Project or PR. Files reached through symlinks remain read-only, with drafts preserved when access changes.\n- Show interactive Flow stages with links to their exact current conversations, plus inspectable automated steps and detailed branches and repeats.\n- Open repository-qualified Task links despite unrelated planning failures, scope Project ownership checks to each repository, and use consistent readable Wave titles.\n\n## Checks\n\nRecorded local verification includes Rust Session association tests, Swift navigation/store/DTO tests, formatting, Clippy and native builds. These checks were not rerun for this description.\n\n[Review evidence and remaining limits](docs/reviews/task-workspace.md) distinguishes fixture coverage from configured demos. Live provider continuation, remote Home association, full cross-Task retention and measured presentation latency remain unverified end to end. Broader independent-operation proposals are documented, not implemented here.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough; not performed end to end:\n\nOpen a Task with multiple Sessions, Option-click a second Session, start a shell and edit a file with autosave disabled. Switch Tasks and return: the split layout, running shell and unfinished draft should remain. Hide and reopen a Session pane to confirm that visibility does not end its conversation.","files":[],"additions":5512,"deletions":1041,"changed_files":99,"merge_commit":"2f14e44224cac531388712c088b03d32ce1a6adf"},{"number":1374,"title":"Keep chapter sweeps and planning refresh working around other Teams’ Projects","body":"A Project owned by another Team could block an entire chapter sweep or planning refresh when it appeared in a linked Linear Initiative. These operations now skip those Projects so repository work can continue, and Wave sync leaves their names untouched.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Let the Chapter sweep skip Projects owned by other Teams · LOO-359](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-359/let-the-chapter-sweep-skip-projects-owned-by-other-teams)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Skip Projects whose known Teams exclude the repository Team before ownership validation, title normalization, and issue enumeration.\n- Report each skipped Project once in sweep output, including its ID and Teams. Project-level JSON entries use `issue: null`; consumers must handle this alongside issue identifiers.\n- Apply the same filtering during planning refresh and both sync inspection and rename execution.\n- Keep ownership errors for Projects with missing Teams or ownership shared with the repository Team. Existing cancellation safeguards remain in place.\n\n## Checks\n\nRecorded gate results: Rust build, formatting, Clippy, architecture checks, and 91 affected Rust tests passed. The stateful GraphQL simulation covers refresh, sync without foreign Project renames, sweep preview/apply, repeated apply, and ambiguous ownership.\n\nLive Linear preview and cancellation remain unverified; no live issues were canceled during implementation or gate. The full matrix remains with CI.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough, not yet performed against live Linear: in a repository whose linked Initiative includes another Team’s Project, run `lf task sweep`. The preview should name that Project once as skipped and continue listing eligible repository issues instead of stopping with an ownership error.","files":[],"additions":217,"deletions":27,"changed_files":5,"merge_commit":"746418d8d934d9c9c6f8830f3e95cdd06d8d1df2"},{"number":1375,"title":"Nest Waves with shared context and stable identity across moves","body":"Waves could not reliably inherit a parent’s context while keeping their own work attached through directory moves. Nested Waves now read ancestor goals and memory while retaining their identity, Project and Task links when renamed or reparented.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Subwaves: nested Waves with their own memory and plan · LOO-354](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-354/subwaves-nested-waves-with-their-own-memory-and-plan)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Derive addresses such as `infrastructure/release` from directory parents. Registration writes a durable `id:` into `GOAL.md`; discovery preserves that identity across moves and fresh Homes.\n- Migrate existing Wave paths into parent relationships while preserving IDs and retired history.\n- Read repository memory and ancestor-to-child Wave Markdown from the executing checkout through one shared context pipeline. Ordinary Wave context excludes siblings and descendants.\n- Share one configurable memory budget across repository, ancestor and selected Wave memory. Oversized files become linked excerpts, and `lf context` reports each file’s allocation and usage.\n- Give Release its own authored goal and release schedule beneath Infrastructure. Teach `realign` to consult child Wave documents when curating parent memory.\n\nRelease’s live Linear ownership, Task transfers and installed schedule synchronization remain separate follow-up operations. Commit generated `id:` fields and retain them when moving Wave directories.\n\n## Checks\n\nAdded coverage for rename/reparent identity, fresh-Home discovery, migration history, context ordering and shared memory limits. The branch records a passing focused memory-budget regression test; broader checks remain with gate/CI. Tests were not rerun for this description.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough, not performed for this description: inspect context for `infrastructure/release`. Infrastructure’s goals and memory should appear before Release’s, without sibling Wave documents. Run `lf context` with that Wave selected to inspect the shared memory allocations.","files":[],"additions":1456,"deletions":1342,"changed_files":63,"merge_commit":"963bf8e0a921763664d6e22640bcbfdb3c0468b5"},{"number":1376,"title":"Inspect and configure context budgets before launching agents","body":"Context limits were fixed in code, making it difficult to see why launch input was excerpted or rejected. This PR exposes effective limits, their configuration sources, and original versus submitted usage so operators can adjust budgets and identify notes that need curation.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Make context budgets visible, configurable and actively kept · LOO-356](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-356/make-context-budgets-visible-configurable-and-actively-kept)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Add `lf context` with Wave, Task, skill, and JSON options. Preview uses local context without launching a provider, refreshing Linear, or reading the clipboard; total-input overages remain inspectable.\n- Configure token and byte limits for memory, scratch, goals, and total input. Each field resolves from Wave frontmatter, repository config, personal config, then defaults.\n- Include budget feedback in launch prompts while preserving complete excerpted sources on disk. Update `realign`, `compress`, `kickoff`, and `implement` to curate oversized notes and recheck usage.\n- Read Wave memory from the execution checkout so local edits reach the next launch. Apply configured goal limits to live direction as well.\n\nThe preview measures the selected skill’s headless input; other launch surfaces can differ, and provider-loaded native instruction files are outside its measurement. Semantic preservation during real memory curation remains unverified.\n\n## Checks\n\nRecorded branch checks report 2,028 Rust tests and 78 website tests passing. The initial Wave registry and architecture failures were repaired and their focused checks passed. Formatting, Clippy, and diff checks passed. These checks were not rerun for this description.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough: run `lf context --skill implement`, set `context_budgets.scratch_tokens: 12000` in the existing repository `.lf/config.yaml`, then rerun the query. The report should show the new limit, its configuration path, and scratch usage before and after any excerpting. Shorten scratch notes and query again to see usage fall.","files":[],"additions":1032,"deletions":80,"changed_files":27,"merge_commit":"35679206755fb7c57592ce0381464e713cba4bf5"},{"number":1377,"title":"Consolidate repository instructions in AGENTS.md","body":"Repository conventions lived in `STYLE.md`, reached through two instruction-file symlinks and referenced under different names. This PR gives maintainers and agents one regular `AGENTS.md` file to read and update, preserving the guide’s contents.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Use AGENTS.md as the one repository instruction file · LOO-363](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-363/use-agentsmd-as-the-one-repository-instruction-file)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Replace the `AGENTS.md` symlink with the existing guide; remove `STYLE.md` and the `CLAUDE.md` symlink.\n- Update builtin skills, documentation, fixtures, and the architecture scanner to reference `AGENTS.md`.\n- Clarify that providers load repository instructions natively and Loopflow excludes them from injected context.\n- Add regression coverage for native-file exclusion and symlink aliases in either direction. Runtime filtering remains unchanged, including support for other repositories using `CLAUDE.md`.\n\n## Checks\n\nRecorded branch verification:\n\n- Fresh headless sessions through `lf` with Claude Code 2.1.286 and Codex CLI 0.159.0 correctly recalled repository conventions without tools or file reads.\n- Native-exclusion tests, the builtin prompt golden, architecture checks, Python tests, website tests, formatting, Clippy, and `git diff --check` passed.\n- **Full Rust verification remains incomplete.** The affected-suite runner stopped after sustained macOS `syspolicyd` CPU usage. Before interruption, the unchanged process-group cleanup test failed because a provider descendant survived termination. It passed in isolation, but the cause remains unresolved; CI still needs to complete the suite and verify that failure.","files":[],"additions":613,"deletions":589,"changed_files":29,"merge_commit":"9eb180b2bc4d35390b8b24ed732fd761cf84c6f8"},{"number":1378,"title":"Deliver Wave context once and include child lessons in realign","body":"Wave context could be repeated in assembled prompts or omitted from IDE skill launches. This PR delivers the complete selected Wave goal once and teaches `realign` to consult child memories when updating a parent’s guidance.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Audit context allocation and subwave memory composition · LOO-331](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-331/audit-context-allocation-and-subwave-memory-composition)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Use assembled prompts for IDE launches with Wave documents so the provider receives their contents.\n- Deduplicate repeated document sources across reference documents and changed files, including symlink aliases. Preserve distinct memory files even when their text matches, and avoid repeating builtin operating guidance.\n- Record why documents were deduplicated and attribute `GOAL.md` to its delivered document rather than also to the Wave seed.\n- Instruct `realign` to discover immediate-child memories, including directories without goals or registry entries, promote shared lessons into the selected parent, and keep local details in their owning files. Unread coverage must remain explicit; this is agent guidance, not automatic memory merging.\n\n## Checks\n\nThe installation compiler repair passed `task-installation` on watched head `4ed06e30012e2dadeaf1e380c8744326f80c5865`. That head's remaining `rust-test` failure was the taskless batch-launch test reading usage from `events.jsonl` before the asynchronous recorder drained. Completion waits at most 250 ms for telemetry.\n\nThis repair removes redundant event-log assertions from the launch test. It retains provider execution, retry count and identity, manifest and terminal receipts, and the absence of planning Tasks. Existing recorder tests cover usage and account events; production recorder behavior is unchanged. TESTING.md now explains the boundary and how to reproduce it.\n\nLocal verification:\n- Reproduced the exact missing-usage assertion by temporarily delaying usage recording by one second; the revised launch test passed with the same delay. Removed the instrumentation before final checks.\n- `cargo nextest run -p loopflow --lib -E 'test(lf::commands::run::tests::) | test(session_record::tests::) | test(engine::stream::tests::)' --no-fail-fast -j 4`: all 95 selected tests passed.\n- `cargo fmt --all -- --check`: passed.\n- `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`: passed with Rust/Clippy 1.98.\n- `git diff --check`: passed.\n\nReview: inspected the full branch diff and the repair. The launch proof now uses synchronous evidence for its assertions; no retry, sleep, timeout increase, or production behavior change is included. New-head CI remains pending publication.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough, not performed: run `realign` for a parent Wave whose child memory contains a broadly useful lesson. Review the resulting edits: the parent should gain the shared lesson, child-specific detail should stay in the child, and any unread memories should be identified.","files":[],"additions":429,"deletions":154,"changed_files":22,"merge_commit":"237d76c49fad1326a8fccd108435235836efc58b"},{"number":1379,"title":"Show all Task checkout work and preserve independent activity","body":"Work started directly in a Task’s checkout could be missing from its status and Desktop navigation. This PR brings that work into view and prevents Task completion or cleanup from discarding a checkout while independent work remains unfinished or unresolved.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [A Task is its worktree's work; the managed FlowSession is a special case · LOO-358](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-358/a-task-is-its-worktrees-work-the-managed-flowsession-is-a-special-case)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Use one Rust association reader for Sessions, Flows and Execs in the checkout and its subdirectories, plus explicitly bound work. Include headless and completed history, even after checkout removal.\n- Add a Desktop Work section and associate Sessions with Tasks through shared membership data. Mark the managed Flow while preserving existing worker authority and historical usage attribution.\n- Make completion, abandonment and cleanup account for independent unfinished Flows, pending Asks/reviews and unresolved execution. Recovery preserves idle Flows and waits for unresolved execution, including worker claims before their first step.\n- Replace Task snapshot JSON’s `runs` and `runs_truncated` fields with `work`; add `task_ids` to Session records and update Rust/Swift consumers and fixtures.\n\n## Checks\n\nAdded coverage for checkout membership, independent Flow preservation, recovery claims, DTO round trips, Desktop rendering and navigation. Tests and lint were not run for this description review.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough; not performed: start a conversation directly inside an existing Task’s checkout, then open that Task in Desktop. The conversation should appear under the Task and in its Work section. Run `lf task status <issue> --json` to inspect the associated Sessions, Flows and Execs, with the managed Flow marked.","files":[],"additions":1051,"deletions":65,"changed_files":44,"merge_commit":"e72e38489a051e02be147fcfa0ee903429e0a974"},{"number":1380,"title":"Keep larger Wave memories intact and curate only what exceeds budget","body":"Wave memory was excerpted above 8,000 tokens or 64 KiB, cutting off useful context such as Product’s memory. This PR doubles those defaults and directs gradual curation to stop once memory fits, preserving more context without unnecessary rewriting.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Loosen the memory budget for a gradual transition · LOO-362](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-362/loosen-the-memory-budget-for-a-gradual-transition)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Raise default Wave memory limits to 16,000 tokens and 128 KiB. Raising both avoids leaving otherwise acceptable memory constrained by the old byte cap.\n- Update `realign`, budget feedback, and docs to retire the largest stale sections first, preserve live decisions and evidence, and avoid shrinking in-budget memory merely for size.\n- Preserve explicit overrides and the total input limits of 64,000 tokens and 512 KiB. No memory files are changed.\n\n## Checks\n\nRecorded gate results: 138 focused Rust tests passed; formatting, Clippy, architecture checks, and diff checks passed. Skill alignment tests passed (4); website tests passed (78, with 3 skipped). The full Rust matrix remains CI-owned.\n\nRegression coverage checks memory at and above the 16,000-token boundary and retains total-input enforcement. Prompt goldens verify the curation instructions; live agent curation judgment has not been demonstrated.\n\n## Try it\n\nRun `target/debug/lf context --wave product --json` to inspect original and submitted memory usage. The recorded preview retained all 14,189 tokens / 66,111 bytes of Product memory. Running the same command with `--wave infrastructure` still excerpted its oversized 23,709-token memory, confirming that larger outliers remain bounded.","files":[],"additions":51,"deletions":14,"changed_files":5,"merge_commit":"708d4e94682ba103ba338420588f0a16d8cc6362"},{"number":1381,"title":"Keep ordinary commands and their child processes on one main Home","body":"Source builds could read a private data copy while Task operations ran against the installed Home, leaving commands showing different work. Ordinary commands now use the installed CLI and `~/.lf`, keeping Task workers, Flow steps, sessions and agent tools on the same Home.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [One main Home that every lf uses · LOO-342](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-342/one-main-home-that-every-lf-uses)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 leaves the Task open for another serial PR.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Forward source CLI commands to the installed CLI before opening a store, and remove branch-copy routing and retired `LF_CONTROL_*` overrides.\n- Make local builds explicit experiments: a custom `LF_HOME` starts empty, initializes once and requires a fresh directory after schema changes. Children inherit that experiment.\n- Remove local promotion, retained development-Home selection and experimental upgrade/recovery paths. Published installation retains snapshot validation, main-store migrations and interrupted-install recovery.\n- Update CLI documentation and fixtures for the new Home selection rules.\n\nExisting side-store data is not imported. Retirement of stores still used by older processes remains post-release work; this PR does not establish installed acceptance.\n\n## Checks\n\n- Recorded post-sync check: the focused Flow-step regression passed, confirming that an explicit Home survives stale control pins and a PATH without `lf`.\n- Added coverage for experimental schema preservation and nested default routing. The default-routing fixture uses real source CLI processes with a simulated installed executable; it is not live installation acceptance.\n- No tests rerun for this description.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough, not performed here: with `LF_HOME` unset and a published CLI installed, run `lf wave list --json`, then `local-bin/lf wave list --json`. Both should show the main Home’s Waves. Run `LF_HOME=\"$(mktemp -d)\" local-bin/lf wave list --json` to see an empty experimental Home instead.","files":[],"additions":818,"deletions":5028,"changed_files":64,"merge_commit":"6c73356074c47a90491c1ed4de8e063d85eb3a30"},{"number":1382,"title":"Keep Tasks and PR delivery progressing with scheduled repository checks","body":"Task recovery needed manual attention, and PR landing kept a watcher running through CI and merge. This PR adds scheduled repository checks that resume enrolled Tasks, launch CI repairs, and settle verified merges while Desktop is closed.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Keep Tasks progressing to landing without a resident or watcher · LOO-332](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-332/keep-tasks-progressing-to-landing-without-a-resident-or-watcher)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Add a per-Home minute schedule with Task enrollment and Hold controls. Checks resume saved Flows, respect pending reviews and live work, and bound retries for unchanged failures. Historical Tasks remain unenrolled until selected.\n- Make `lf land` return after recording delivery. `lf task reconcile` checks Tasks and landings once; `lf pr reconcile` checks delivery alone. A Flow’s landing step still waits for verified merge before advancing.\n- Run CI repairs in detached Sessions with durable admission and recovery. Confirm failures before repair, avoid repeating completed repairs for unchanged evidence, and bound timeout reruns for pending or missing checks.\n- Retry interrupted Task settlement and retain release worktrees through repair and merge.\n- Show schedule coverage, recent errors, Task blockers, and pending deliveries in Desktop’s Background progress panel and `lf task automation --json`.\n\nScheduling uses macOS launchd and requires the placed Home’s user to remain logged in. Holding a Task or disabling the schedule prevents future automatic admissions; running work and existing GitHub merge requests continue.\n\n## Checks\n\nAdded coverage for overlapping checks, holds and reviews, retry exhaustion, landing handoff, repair recovery, release finalization, and Rust/Swift DTO parity. Provider and launch behavior use simulations. Tests were not rerun for this description.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough, not performed here: run `lf cron sync --repo`, then enroll an existing Task with `lf task automate <issue> on`. Close Desktop and reopen it after a minute. Background progress should show a recent check and either resumed work or the reason the Task is waiting. Select Hold to prevent its next automatic admission.","files":[],"additions":3651,"deletions":1501,"changed_files":60,"merge_commit":"93395e2c0c36fe6bfd254fae9104b8de92a1753e"},{"number":1383,"title":"Give each repository and Wave one ongoing conversation","body":"Returning to repository or Wave work meant choosing which conversation to resume. Each now has a primary Session that can be found repeatedly, continued across days, or replaced with a fresh conversation while preserving its history.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Primary Sessions: repository, Wave and Task conversations · LOO-364](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-364/primary-sessions-repository-wave-and-task-conversations)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Add `lf session ensure` for the repository’s conversation and `lf session ensure -w <wave>` for a Wave’s. Repository selection needs no Wave, Task, or planning provider and ignores ambient Wave context.\n- Add `lf session replace SESSION` to stop the previous provider and atomically complete its Session and admit a successor. Repeating the request returns the current successor; failed launches can retry with the same Session.\n- Enforce one uncompleted primary Session per scope through SQLite unique indexes and serialize creation and replacement across processes.\n- Add repository and Wave conversation skills that reconcile work, surface pending decisions, and develop direction while existing Task Flows retain execution. Wave conversations receive the Wave’s goal and memory; primary status grants no additional Flow or review authority.\n\n## Checks\n\nAdded coverage for Session reuse, launch retries, replacement, repositories without Waves, and explicit versus ambient Wave selection. Provider launches and stops are simulated in these tests. Tests and lint were not run for this description.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough, not performed: run `lf session ensure` twice in a repository; both responses should name the same Session. Open it with `lf session connect SESSION`. Run `lf session replace SESSION`, then ensure again: the returned ID should be the successor’s, with the original conversation retained as completed history.","files":[],"additions":716,"deletions":15,"changed_files":15,"merge_commit":"5eaf887fe01dab9655fea983081d66da959e5603"},{"number":1384,"title":"Watch GitHub CI and start repairs from Desktop or the CLI","body":"Failed PR checks previously relied on scheduled reconciliation to start repairs. Loopflow Desktop now runs a dedicated CI watcher for each open repository, with the same watcher available in a terminal or as a background service.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Desktop watches GitHub CI and wakes the right TaskSession · LOO-365](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-365/desktop-watches-github-ci-and-wakes-the-right-tasksession)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Add `lf ci watch` with one-pass, service installation, and status options. Poll GitHub roughly once a minute with conditional requests and rate-limit backoff.\n- Route failed recorded landings through the existing confirmation and repair reservation, preventing duplicate repairs. Additional watcher processes stand by and take over when the active watcher exits.\n- Start and stop Desktop watchers with repository windows. Record check completion times so `lf ci` can report detection latency.\n- Make scheduled reconciliation and `lf pr reconcile` record failures and settle merges without starting repairs. Release-owned landings retain their repair path.\n\nRepairs require a recorded landing, including standalone landings without a Task; other failing PRs are reported only. Without a watcher, ordinary landing failures wait. Repair confirmation still requires GraphQL quota, and watcher status is available through the CLI but has no Desktop view yet.\n\n## Checks\n\nBranch notes report passing Rust formatting, Clippy, focused watcher and landing tests, delivery integration tests, and Swift watcher lifecycle tests. These were not rerun for this description. A live GitHub polling pass was recorded; repair and merge behavior was verified against simulated GitHub, not a real failing landing.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough: open a repository in Desktop, then run `lf ci watch --status` from that repository. It should show a running watcher and, after its first poll, PR check status. For a recorded landing whose required checks fail, subsequent status should show the ci-fix repair or the reason it could not start.","files":[],"additions":1952,"deletions":58,"changed_files":29,"merge_commit":"3d1f76780a21c8269a9fbb51188ea0c6e3486991"},{"number":1385,"title":"Reuse one migration draft per Task as the schema evolves","body":"Repeated migration requests previously created separate drafts, leaving maintainers to manage intermediate schemas that had never shipped. The migration helper now points back to the branch’s existing draft so a Task’s schema changes can be edited in one place.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Keep one migration draft per Task, edited in place · LOO-344](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-344/keep-one-migration-draft-per-task-edited-in-place)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Detect drafts added since the branch’s merge base with main and reuse the existing file without changing its SQL.\n- Name drafts `<name>.sql`, removing generated IDs and redundant headers while preserving dependency ordering at release.\n- Remove the experimental Home draft receipt ledger; retain released-migration checks and exact-schema validation.\n- Update design and implementation guidance to edit unreleased migrations in place and test upgrades from the released schema to the finished draft. Refresh the implementation prompt golden and document the required golden check.\n\nExisting experimental Homes containing the removed ledger require a fresh `LF_HOME`. Draft edits are not upgrades for existing experimental Homes.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough, not performed: on a new Task branch, run `uv run python scripts/new_migration.py add_wave_colour` and add SQL to the printed file. Then run `uv run python scripts/new_migration.py revise_wave_colour`. The helper should print the original draft’s path, preserve its SQL, and create no second file.","files":[],"additions":280,"deletions":765,"changed_files":34,"merge_commit":"3570076e9e4ea9da1d947a6a81e058130d09f27d"},{"number":1386,"title":"Keep one database per Home and clear stale session context","body":"Separate database overrides made Home selection inconsistent across tools, while tmux could carry an earlier Run’s identity into unrelated sessions. This PR gives each Home one database and prevents newly started tmux servers from inheriting the launching command’s execution context.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Audit lf environment variables against the policies they implement · LOO-341](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-341/audit-lf-environment-variables-against-the-policies-they-implement)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Always use `$LF_HOME/loopflow.db`; remove `LF_DB_PATH` support and update child launches, schedules, scripts, and fixtures.\n- Use one process-context list to clear inherited state from session shells and tmux clients. Expand Desktop’s cleanup of inherited execution context.\n- Resolve global configuration through the shared Home resolver.\n- Remove obsolete environment-variable plumbing and document retained variables, readers, and policies in `docs/architecture/environment.md`.\n\nExisting custom database paths are no longer selected or migrated. Already-running tmux servers retain their inherited environment. Three callers still use `LF_RUN_ID` presence without validating the Run; that remains follow-up work.\n\n## Checks\n\nRecorded branch validation, not rerun for this description:\n\n- Clippy, five focused Rust integration suites, and four affected Python test files passed.\n- Rust library tests: 1,583/1,586 passed with ambient `LF_*` removed. The three migration failures were also reproduced on `main`.\n- Full matrix remains for gate/CI.","files":[],"additions":333,"deletions":863,"changed_files":80,"merge_commit":"825b41dbb61966b8a212f7cc5b14d3d8564185da"},{"number":1387,"title":"Show each step’s context by source and flag budget overruns","body":"Understanding why a step used so much context previously required reconstructing its captured input by hand. This PR shows the source breakdown in the CLI and Desktop Task history, making large inputs and budget overruns easier to spot.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Show what each step's context is made of · LOO-347](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-347/show-what-each-steps-context-is-made-of)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Add `lf usage --context` for per-step breakdowns and source totals, with Task filtering and JSON output. Session inspection also includes context details; `lf mon show SESSION --context` isolates them.\n- Break out instructions, memory, scratch, goal, steers, other assembled input, carried context, tool output and compaction evidence. New Task launches record steer counts and author references separately from the goal.\n- Show source summaries and Task totals in Desktop Session history.\n- Read existing captures without adding a store. Flag memory, scratch, goal, steers and assembled input against current checkout budgets; expose missing measurements as unknown.\n\nCounts use different tokenizers: assembled input and tool output use cl100k, while request and compaction counts come from providers. Carried context is an estimate that includes provider preamble and tool definitions; it is unavailable for Codex. Older captures retain steers inside the goal.\n\n## Checks\n\nBranch notes record passing focused Rust context, attribution and DTO tests, formatting and Clippy, plus prior Swift build and DTO validation. These checks were not rerun for this description.\n\nThe reader was exercised directly against real Claude and Codex captures. End-to-end CLI validation remains blocked by the installed store’s incompatibility with the branch binary. Desktop appearance remains unreviewed, and compaction handling has only simulated coverage.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough: run `lf usage --task <TASK-ID> --context` for a Task with retained captures. Each recorded step should show source counts, with `*` marking budget overruns. Inspect a listed input with `lf mon show <INPUT> --context` to see available steer attribution and explanations of missing evidence.","files":[],"additions":1442,"deletions":9,"changed_files":20,"merge_commit":"54cccf952b825e0fddc81b1c3959477d77d9bf42"},{"number":1388,"title":"Track weekly context cost and turn latency with lf usage","body":"Comparing context use and slow turns previously required manual analysis of run records. `lf usage --weekly` now reports repeatable weekly trends so maintainers can evaluate prompt changes against recorded token use and turn time.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Track context cost and turn latency over time · LOO-348](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-348/track-context-cost-and-turn-latency-over-time)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Report input:output ratios and input tokens per captured Session input, grouped by skill, harness and Task, with text and JSON output.\n- Show median and p90 turn duration by harness and peak-input band. Count silent gaps over ten minutes only inside completed turns, excluding waits between turns.\n- Anchor weeks to the baseline week ending September 30, 2026, mark open weeks, and exclude missing token usage from ratios.\n- Add four Intelligence Wave metric contracts and publish the latest complete week from unfiltered runs, including through `lf telemetry-daily`. Wave, Project and Task filters remain read-only; publication failures leave the report available.\n\nChapter KRs and targets are documented but remain unapplied. The historical silence baseline covers a different population and needs comparison with the report’s baseline before becoming a weekly target.\n\n## Checks\n\nBranch notes record five focused tests passing and `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` passing; these were not rerun for this description. Live-data validation remains outstanding because Jack’s installed store predates `session_events`; runtime verification used an empty Home.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough, not performed against live data: run `lf usage --weekly` with a compatible store containing recorded Session inputs. Expect weekly totals followed by the latest populated week’s skill, harness, Task and turn-time breakdowns. Add `--task <Task ID>` to inspect one Task without publishing metrics.","files":[],"additions":866,"deletions":2,"changed_files":15,"merge_commit":"d3587939774f7e002864967f79278cfcafc33e2a"},{"number":1390,"title":"Find Sessions waiting for review or reply and retire exited orphan conversations","body":"Conversations needing attention could be hidden among open Sessions or behind collapsed Tasks, while exited orphan conversations kept cluttering navigation. This PR adds a shared “Needs me” view in Desktop and the CLI, and removes confirmed exited orphan conversations from the working set while retaining their history.\n\n## What changes\n\n- Add Desktop’s **Needs me** filter and `lf session list --needs-me`, including headless Asks and current reviews. Search filters Desktop destinations and their count together; failed reads show an unavailable count.\n- Make individual Sessions reachable beneath Tasks in every navigation presentation, and place repository primary conversations at the top.\n- Record driver exits and interruptions. Retire unassigned, non-primary conversations only on confirmed normal or interrupted exits from their owning driver; preserve Task/Wave conversations, primary Sessions, Asks, and Flow reviews. Stopping a response or losing process evidence does not close a conversation or settle a Flow.\n- Prevent retained Flow cursors on terminal Tasks from creating stale review obligations, while keeping independent unresolved Asks discoverable.\n- Correct orphan test fixtures to launch outside registered Task checkouts, avoiding macOS path aliases that masked membership on Linux.\n\n## Checks\n\nAdded coverage for attention pagination, exact Session navigation, interruption receipts, orphan retirement, and stale-driver protection. Tests were not run while preparing this description.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough, not performed here: with an unresolved Ask or review, collapse its Task or Wave and select **Needs me**. The conversation should remain visible and open directly. Run `lf session list --needs-me --json` against the same repository and runtime/Home to inspect its review or reply obligation.","files":[],"additions":795,"deletions":165,"changed_files":33,"merge_commit":"62d34344dac5977ef8ea001116a438e6cadeb382"},{"number":1391,"title":"Measure launch context effects and reduce the default scratch budget","body":"Maintainers could measure launch context size but lacked comparisons showing what agents did when a source was removed. This PR adds repeatable comparisons and a documented pilot, and lowers the default scratch allowance by 25%.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Measure which context changes outcomes · LOO-349](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-349/measure-which-context-changes-outcomes)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Add `scripts/context_ablation.py` to inventory recorded context, replay variants in disposable Homes and checkouts, and compare decisions, changed files, time, and provider usage against baseline repeats.\n- Preserve findings and aggregate data from 95 launches and 31 replays across eight recorded steps in `performance/context-ablation.md` and its baseline files.\n- Reduce scratch defaults from 16,000 to 12,000 tokens and from 128 to 96 KiB. Memory, goal, and total-input budgets remain unchanged; scratch files remain available on disk.\n\nThe pilot does not establish equivalent code quality or reliable time and cost savings. It measures fresh launches only, includes approximated checkout commits, and shows substantial variation even between identical baseline replays. Replays require an `lf` build that honors `LF_HOME` and consume provider usage.\n\n## Checks\n\nRecorded branch checks: three focused Python tests passed, Ruff lint and formatting passed, and report output remained byte-identical after simplifying the collector. These checks were not rerun while preparing this description.\n\n## Try it\n\nRun `uv run python -m scripts.context_ablation census` against local recorded launches. The JSON report shows each source’s token share and available turn usage without invoking a provider. This is a suggested walkthrough; it was not performed while preparing this description.","files":[],"additions":1686,"deletions":3,"changed_files":8,"merge_commit":"16f03bb37dae7431081a1e3908a6255defb0b70c"},{"number":1394,"title":"Compare Task and Wave usage under alternative bind attribution rules","body":"Binding a Session to a Task leaves its earlier usage with its original owner, making the impact of assigning the whole conversation to the Task difficult to assess. This PR adds a read-only comparison of recorded totals and hypothetical post-hoc totals without changing attribution.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Re-evaluate usage attribution after a Session is bound to a Task · LOO-336](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-336/re-evaluate-usage-attribution-after-a-session-is-bound-to-a-task)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Add `lf usage --binds` and `--json` output showing usage before, during, and after each bind, alongside affected Task and Wave token and cost totals.\n- Derive hypothetical totals from the captured inputs that would move, preserving existing totals and flagging missing usage measurements.\n- Document the evaluation and recommendation in `performance/bind-attribution.md`: retain prospective attribution until real bind history supports a decision.\n\nThe recorded evaluation found no real binds on Jack Heart’s installation, which lacked Session history support. The frequency and cost impact therefore remain unmeasured.\n\n## Checks\n\nBranch notes record passing focused `bind_attribution` and `bind_preserves_prior_work` tests and `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`. These checks were not rerun while drafting this description.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough, not performed here: with recorded Session bind history, run `lf usage --binds`. Inspect the before/during/after breakdown and compare each affected Task or Wave’s recorded totals with the hypothetical post-hoc totals. Add `--json` to inspect step counts and missing measurements.","files":[],"additions":475,"deletions":2,"changed_files":12,"merge_commit":"3a3656643621b6e5ddf0259123970b72c3792b3c"}],"decisions":null,"previous_release_notes":"# v0.12.29\n\n<!-- loopflow:release-notes=narrative;gate=safe -->\n\nv0.12.29 gives conversations and Flows durable records so reconnecting preserves conversation identity and resuming a Flow uses its captured progress. Task and taskless execution now share one driver, with retries retaining the conversation and history keeping failed-turn evidence. Upgrade for clearer recovery and discoverable history, but review the cutover limits below before converting an existing installation.\n\n## Pick up the same conversation\n\nConversations now retain their identity independently of the command process or client driving them. Discovery and history make it easier to find prior work and understand what happened across reconnects.\n\n- Find conversations with `lf session list`, name one with `lf session rename SESSION \"Review notes\"`, and reconnect with `lf session connect SESSION`. Read its recorded history with `lf session history SESSION --json`.\n- Codex clients can reconnect without restarting the live engine. Feedback, failed-turn evidence, and original usage attribution are retained.\n- Sessions can be permanently bound to Tasks, including completed Tasks. Desktop preserves terminals and drafts through discovery and pane replacement.\n\n## Resume Flows from recorded progress\n\nTask and taskless Flows use the same driver and captured progression. A retry keeps the conversation, while only the selected successful native completion advances the Flow.\n\n- Skills and mechanical steps execute through ordinary commands. Loop passes remain positions within one FlowSession.\n- Indexed discovery covers Sessions, command executions, and saved Flows.\n- SQLite records give each kind of history an explicit owner: Exec records an actual `lf` process, AgentSession owns a conversation, and FlowSession owns a started Flow. These replace the previous Run owner.\n\n## Carry active work into the next plan\n\nLinear Project status now owns chapter planning and history, and Projects supply the default Flow. Plan rotation preserves started work while limiting retirement to backlog proven untouched.\n\n- Started Tasks survive rotation.\n- Backlog with uncertain evidence is not automatically retired.\n\n## Operational notes\n\n- **Cutover:** historical import and intermediate-schema compatibility have been removed. Finished history is not imported. Installed conversion still requires a consistent database/filesystem backup and rehearsal of current-state retention; see the [cutover status](docs/architecture-reference.md#cutover-status).\n- **Acceptance still open:** configured provider, Desktop, and Linear acceptance remain pending. The Session commands above are a suggested walkthrough, not evidence of completed acceptance.\n- **Validation:** recorded gate evidence includes passing Python and website suites, formatting, and all-target Clippy. Full Rust and Swift runs had failures followed by focused repair checks; those repairs do not establish a clean full run of the final head. See the [retained gate evidence](wave/infrastructure/MEMORY.md#data-model-and-performance-decisions-reconciled-2026-09-30).","source_limits":{"context_bytes":131072,"commits":256,"merged_prs":200,"files_per_change":100,"area_scopes":32,"name_bytes":256,"title_bytes":300,"path_bytes":512,"pr_body_bytes":4096,"decisions_bytes":16384,"previous_release_notes_bytes":16384,"release_notes_bytes":61440},"omissions":{"commits":0,"merged_prs":0,"commit_files":103,"pr_files":0,"area_scopes":0,"text_bytes":0,"decisions_bytes":0,"previous_release_notes_bytes":0}}